@using XgFilter_Lib.Enums

@* ── FilterHelp.razor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Producer-owned documentation for every facet FilterPanel offers, in
   user-level language — behavior only, never FilterConfig internals or field
   names. It lives beside the panel that renders the facets so the prose has
   one owner: consumers embed this component (BgQuiz's Help adds only
   app-level framing around it) and must never write their own facet prose —
   a second description of a facet's semantics is a second encoding that
   silently drifts.

   Render-only and parameterless: no JS, no localStorage, no state. Structured
   for embedding — one section per facet, each heading carrying a stable
   fh-* anchor id, headings from the lib's FilterFacet [Description]s via
   ToLabel() so help titles, panel section headings, and the hidden-active
   signal all name a facet identically. The shelved facets (Position types /
   Play types) are documented only when their UI returns.
   Location: XgFilter_Razor/Components/FilterHelp.razor
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

<div class="filter-help">

    <h4 id="fh-filters">Filters</h4>
    <p>
        Filters select which analyzed decisions are admitted. Every filter you
        set must agree for a decision to pass — the facets combine with AND.
        Within a single facet, checked options combine with OR: checking more
        options admits more decisions. A facet left at rest (nothing typed,
        nothing checked) admits everything, so an empty panel passes every
        decision.
    </p>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-error-range">@FilterFacet.ErrorRange.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions whose error size falls between the two bounds,
            inclusive. Leave a bound blank for no limit on that side. While an
            error range is set, decisions without a measured error are
            excluded.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-players">@FilterFacet.Players.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions made by any of the listed players
            (comma-separated). Blank means all players. A match involving none
            of the listed players is skipped entirely.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-decision-type">@FilterFacet.DecisionType.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Restricts to checker plays only, cube decisions only, or admits
            both.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-match-scores">@FilterFacet.MatchScores.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions taken at any of the listed scores
            (comma-separated). Scores are written <em>MaNa</em> and are
            anchored to the player on roll: M is the points the player on roll
            still needs, N the opponent — so 4a5a and 5a4a are different
            scores; list both to catch either orientation. Append C for a
            Crawford game (1a2aC), write money for money sessions, and note
            double match point is 1a1a.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-move-number-range">@FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions by their move number within the game, between the
            two bounds, inclusive. Leave a bound blank for no limit on that
            side. Games that do not begin from the standard starting position
            are excluded while this filter is active.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-contact-type">@FilterFacet.ContactTypes.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions by the character of the position — contact or
            race. Nothing checked means all types.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-analysis-depth">@FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions by how they were analyzed. There are three modes:
            an <strong>evaluation</strong> (XG's neural-net judgment at some
            ply level), a <strong>rollout</strong> (games played out to
            settle the decision), and a <strong>book rollout</strong> (a
            stored result looked up from XG's opening book).
        </p>
        <p>
            Check a mode to admit its decisions; each checked mode admits its
            decisions independently, so checking more always matches more, and
            with no mode checked the facet is off — every decision passes,
            including older ones whose analysis depth was never recorded.
            While the facet is on, those unrecorded decisions are excluded.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under each checked mode you can narrow by analysis level. Levels
            you check narrow only their own mode; none checked means any level
            for that mode. What "level" means differs by mode: an
            evaluation's level is its own; a rollout's level is the
            <em>inner</em> level its games were played at — checker plays
            never carry XG Roller-family inner levels; a book rollout's level
            is the inner level from the opening-book entry, when the book
            recorded one.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-dice-rolls">@FilterFacet.DiceRolls.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits checker plays made with any of the checked rolls. Dice
            order does not matter — 31 covers 3-1 and 1-3 alike. Nothing
            checked means all rolls. While rolls are checked, cube decisions
            never match: no roll exists before the cube is offered.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h5 id="fh-position-pattern">@FilterFacet.PositionPattern.ToLabel()</h5>
        <p>
            Admits decisions whose position matches a set of per-location
            checker-count ranges, written as a bracket list —
            [location,min,max] per location, with a blank bound meaning
            unbounded. Positive counts belong to the player on roll, negative
            to the opponent. Locations are points 1–24 (numbered from the
            player on roll's perspective), the bars (25 for the player on
            roll, 0 for the opponent), and the borne-off trays (off and
            opp-off). Every listed location must match. Blank means no
            pattern filter.
        </p>
    </section>

</div>
